// What a second host has to answer the same way, opcode by opcode — and what nothing yet checks.
//
// design/system/0001's open questions name this as the largest risk in the direction: "the native core
// is the first large subsystem here with nothing independent to check it against, in a repo whose
// rigour is mostly differential." Since then a good deal *has* been checked — the arrival test moves an
// image between the two hosts, `native_hostfs_test.wac` compares twelve filesystem operations and every
// grant refusal, `native_shell_test.wac` runs a slice of the shell corpus on both — but that coverage
// grew a file at a time, and **nothing knew what it added up to.**
//
// This is the ledger. For every opcode on the two-host surface it names where the comparison lives, or
// says plainly that there is none. It is not a second copy of those tests and it does not re-run them:
// duplicating a differential is how two of them come to disagree.
//
// ## The surface is derived, and the derivation is where the TypeScript tripped
//
// The requests come from std's `OP_*` table — the one every host implements since design/system/0013 step 6 — and
// the native host's from where `native/src/main.rs` uses each `OP_*` name. Until then the opcodes came from
// `host/ops.ts` and the native capabilities from a `("Core", "name")` table, and the two were joined by a
// `SCREAMING_SNAKE` to `camelCase` mapping right 36 times out of 38; now every side spells a request the same way.
// `conformance.wac` holds the scanners and every one of them is asserted to have found enough.
//
// ## Three of the first entries were wrong, which is the argument for writing it down
//
// The table was filled in from memory of tests one agent had written, and then each claim checked
// against the file it named. `OUTPUT_ERROR` and `READ_STDIN` had no comparison at all — every two-host
// runner passed `stdin: "null"`, so the one thing none of them exercised was a program reading its
// input — and `LINK_STAT` was an embellishment: `find .` is compared on both hosts and calls `stat`,
// not `linkStat`. A ledger of remembered coverage is worse than none, because it reads like an audit.
//
// ## Why a ledger rather than more tests
//
// An unaccounted-for surface is the failure this repository keeps finding in its own measurements: a
// suite that covers twelve of thirty-eight and reports twelve passes reads exactly like one that
// covers everything. Naming the twenty-six is worth more than quietly adding a thirteenth, and it is
// what makes adding the thirteenth an obvious next move rather than an idea somebody has to have.
//
// Host-side until 2026-08-19, `issues/system/0161`.

import { T } from "../../../wactest/src/assert.wac";
import { Env, Files, Output, Procs, Sys } from "std";
import { contains } from "../../../wactest/src/strings.wac";
import { bridgeNumbers, has, joined, nativeRequests, requestNumbers, requests, sorted, sourceOf } from "./conformance.wac";

/**
 * Where each opcode's comparison lives, or why there is none.
 *
 * `where` names the **file** that runs the same wac on more than one host and compares; `sameAs` names
 * another opcode whose test covers this one too; a `gap` is an opcode no such test reaches — not an
 * accusation, a work list, and the reason each one is hard is worth as much as the entry itself.
 *
 * ## `test` is a path, and `hostsOf` reads it
 *
 * This carried `compared: bool` until 2026-08-26 and the bool was the defect `issues/system/0268a` is
 * about: it answers *is there a comparison* and is read as *do the hosts agree*. There are four hosts —
 * `native/v8`, `wacland` on wasmtime, Deno, Node — and **no entry here compares all four**. Node is in
 * none of them, which is how `LINK_STAT` and `SET_EXECUTABLE` sat marked covered while the Node host
 * answered a zeroed `Stat` and *this filesystem has no mode bits to set*.
 *
 * **Derived rather than written down**, and that is the whole design. The obvious fix was a host list
 * per entry, filled in by hand from the table in `0268a` — which rebuilds the defect one layer up,
 * because a hand-written host set is remembered coverage and the first entry whose test changes goes
 * silently wrong. Exactly how `LINK_STAT` was wrong twice. So the entry names a file and `hostsOf`
 * reads what that file spawns.
 *
 * A derive can under-report by missing a spelling — mine did, on its first draft, reading
 * `native_shell_test.wac` as Deno-only because it reaches the binary through `nativeBinaryPath` rather
 * than `wacBinary`. What makes it an instrument rather than a heuristic is the floor:
 * `test_every_entry_names_a_file_that_says_which_hosts_it_drives` refuses an entry that derives *no*
 * host at all, so a missed spelling fails loudly instead of quietly shrinking the number.
 *
 * ## What these numbers do not say, and cannot
 *
 * **They are about the tests this file points at.** Every check here verifies that a *cited* file's
 * hosts are credited; nothing can tell that an *uncited* file covers an opcode, because that is a
 * different question and a much harder one.
 *
 * That gap is not theoretical — it was the whole of `issues/system/0268a`. The ledger reported `0
 * include the Node host` while `runtimes_test.wac`, `node_net_test.wac` and `echod_test.wac` compared
 * Deno against Node across seventeen opcodes. Nothing was wrong with the derive; the entries pointed
 * elsewhere. It took reading each of those tests and following into the program it builds.
 *
 * So: **a low figure here is a prompt to go looking, not a finding.** Before concluding a host is
 * uncovered, grep for tests that build for it and check what their programs call through every handle —
 * `WRITE_STDOUT`, `WRITE_STDERR` and `MONOTONIC_NANOS` were missed on the first pass of that walk because
 * the scan looked for `cli.` and half the surface hung off `core.`.
 */
struct Cover {
  string op;
  /** `where`, `sameAs` or `gap`. */
  string kind;
  /** For `where` the test's path; for `sameAs` the opcode it borrows; "" for a gap. */
  string ref;
  /**
   * A **second** test covering the same opcode on other hosts, or "".
   *
   * **One opcode is routinely covered by two tests, and they compare different hosts.**
   * `BIND_DATAGRAM` is `datagram_test.wac` — v8, wasmtime and Deno through the handler table — *and*
   * `echod_test.wac`, which builds a real program for Deno and Node and talks to a foreign UDP peer.
   * Neither is the better test; they answer about different hosts. A single field forces a choice
   * between two true statements and makes the derived total understate whichever is not picked, which
   * is the same shape of quiet wrongness this whole entry structure exists to remove.
   *
   * Hosts are **unioned** across the two, so the number means *compared on this host by something*
   * rather than *by the one test we happened to cite*.
   */
  string also;
  string note;
}

Cover where(string op, string test, string note) { return Cover(op, "where", test, "", note); }
/** A `where` with a second test covering other hosts — see `Cover.also`. */
Cover where2(string op, string test, string also, string note) {
  return Cover(op, "where", test, also, note);
}
/**
 * This opcode is exercised by the test another opcode names.
 *
 * **Data rather than the sentence it was.** Four entries read `"as CONNECT — the same program, the
 * same run"`, which nothing checked: no test asserted a `CONNECT` entry existed, so a rename would
 * have left four rows pointing at nothing and still counting as covered.
 */
Cover sameAs(string op, string other, string note) { return Cover(op, "sameAs", other, "", note); }
/**
 * A `sameAs` that a second test also covers directly.
 *
 * `LISTEN` and `ACCEPT` borrow `CONNECT`'s program, and are *separately* compared on Deno and Node by
 * `node_net_test.wac` — which builds `greet.wac` for both targets, and that program calls `listen`,
 * `accept` and `send`. `CONNECT` itself is not: the connecting side there is the test harness's own
 * client, not the program under comparison.
 */
Cover sameAs2(string op, string other, string also, string note) {
  return Cover(op, "sameAs", other, also, note);
}
Cover gap(string op, string note) { return Cover(op, "gap", "", "", note); }

/** Whether an entry claims a comparison at all — `where` or `sameAs`, not `gap`. */
bool isCompared(Cover c) { return c.kind != "gap"; }

/**
 * Whether `src` calls `name` itself, not a longer name ending in it. `binaryPath(` is the tail of
 * `nativeBinaryPath(`, which answers the *wasmtime* binary, so a bare substring credited every file
 * that drives wasmtime through that helper with the V8 host as well.
 */
bool hasCall(string src, string name) {
  u8[] bytes = src.toBytes();
  i32 from = 0;
  while (true) {
    i32 found = src.slice(from, src.len()).indexOf(name);
    if (found < 0) { return false; }
    i32 at = from + found;
    if (at == 0) { return true; }
    u8 before = bytes[at - 1];
    bool ident = (before >= 97 && before <= 122) || (before >= 65 && before <= 90)
                 || (before >= 48 && before <= 57) || before == 95;
    if (!ident) { return true; }
    from = at + 1;
  }
}

/**
 * The hosts a backing test actually drives, as a sorted `+`-joined list, or "" if it drives none.
 *
 * Keyed on **call-shaped** patterns rather than bare words: `"deno"` appears in comments in most of
 * these files, and matching it would report every one of them as covering the Deno host. Three
 * spellings reach the V8 binary and two reach Deno, which is why this is a list rather than an `if`.
 */
string hostsOfFile(Output err, Env env, Files fs, Procs procs, string path) {
  string src = sourceOf(fs, path);
  string out = "";
  // `nativeBinaryPath` and `requireNativeHost` answer the wasmtime binary too, and the second does not
  // contain `nativeHost(` — the capital is in the way — so each is named rather than left to a substring.
  if (src.indexOf("nativeHost(") >= 0 || src.indexOf("requireNativeHost(") >= 0
      || src.indexOf("nativeBinaryPath(") >= 0) { out = out + "wasmtime"; }
  // **Four spellings, and the literal path is the one that matters most.** `native_test.wac` reaches
  // the V8 binary as `root(env) + "/native/v8/target/release/wac"` — no helper — and with only the
  // three helper names this file derived it as wasmtime-only, against a note that says in as many
  // words *"run by both native binaries"*. The floor test below is what surfaced that; the fix is a
  // fourth pattern rather than a fourth guess.
  if (src.indexOf("wacBinary(") >= 0 || hasCall(src, "binaryPath(")
      || src.indexOf("native/v8/target/release/wac") >= 0) {
    out = out + (out == "" ? "" : "+") + "v8";
  }
  if (src.indexOf("denoBatch(") >= 0 || src.indexOf("builtByDeno(") >= 0
      || src.indexOf("\"--target\", \"deno\"") >= 0 || src.indexOf("cli.exec(\"deno\"") >= 0) {
    out = out + (out == "" ? "" : "+") + "deno";
  }
  // **`cli.exec("node"` is the one that matters**, and the literal `--target node` is not. Every test
  // that drives Node builds through a helper taking the target as a *variable* —
  // `build(t, err, env, fs, procs, WC, nodeOut, "node", …)` — so the flag pair never appears in the source at all.
  // Three tests derived as Deno-only for that reason, and the floor below could not see it: an entry
  // that finds one host of two passes a check that only refuses finding none.
  if (src.indexOf("cli.exec(\"node\"") >= 0 || src.indexOf("\"--target\", \"node\"") >= 0
      || src.indexOf("builtByNode(") >= 0) {
    out = out + (out == "" ? "" : "+") + "node";
  }
  return out;}

/** `hostsOfFile` for an entry, following one `sameAs` hop. */
string hostsOf(Output err, Env env, Files fs, Procs procs, Cover[] all, Cover c) {
  if (c.kind == "gap") { return ""; }
  if (c.kind == "where") {
    string first = hostsOfFile(err, env, fs, procs, c.ref);
    if (c.also == "") { return first; }
    // Unioned, not concatenated: the second test usually repeats a host the first already had, and
    // `contains(hosts, "deno")` must not depend on how many times it appears.
    string second = hostsOfFile(err, env, fs, procs, c.also);
    string[] names = ["wasmtime", "v8", "deno", "node"];
    string out = "";
    for (i32 i = 0; i < names.len(); i++) {
      if (contains(first, names[i]) || contains(second, names[i])) {
        out = out + (out == "" ? "" : "+") + names[i];
      }
    }
    return out;
  }
  for (i32 i = 0; i < all.len(); i++) {
    // One hop only: a `sameAs` naming a `sameAs` would be a chain nobody can read, and there is no
    // reason to write one.
    //
    // **`hostsOf` and not `hostsOfFile`**, so the borrowed entry's *second* test comes with it.
    // `RECEIVE_FROM` and `SEND_TO` borrow `BIND_DATAGRAM`, which is covered by `datagram_test.wac`
    // and by `echod_test.wac` on different hosts; reading only the first would have given the two
    // borrowers a narrower answer than the entry they name, which is a disagreement inside the
    // ledger rather than a fact about the hosts.
    if (all[i].op == c.ref && all[i].kind == "where") {
      string inherited = hostsOf(err, env, fs, procs, all, all[i]);
      if (c.also == "") { return inherited; }
      string mine = hostsOfFile(err, env, fs, procs, c.also);
      string[] names = ["wasmtime", "v8", "deno", "node"];
      string out = "";
      for (i32 k = 0; k < names.len(); k++) {
        if (contains(inherited, names[k]) || contains(mine, names[k])) {
          out = out + (out == "" ? "" : "+") + names[k];
        }
      }
      return out;
    }
  }
  return "";
}

Cover[] coverage() {
  return [
    // ── The filesystem: twelve operations and every grant refusal, on both hosts ──
    where2("READ_FILE", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_hostfs: `cat f`, and refused with no grant"),
    where2("WRITE_FILE", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_hostfs: `touch`, and write-only grants"),
    where2("STAT", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_hostfs: `stat f`"),
    where("LINK_STAT", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac",
      "native_hostfs: `test -h` over a real symlink and a real dangling one, against GNU on both " +
      "hosts, with `test -e` beside each — the pair is what says these are two different calls " +
      "rather than one answering for both. The fixture makes the links itself because there is no " +
      "`ln` in this system, so no script could make its own; that absence is why this sat as a gap " +
      "while `Fs.linkStat` had two callers"),
    where2("READ_DIR", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_hostfs: `ls`"),
    where("MKDIR", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "native_hostfs: `mkdir d2; ls; rmdir d2`"),
    where2("REMOVE", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_hostfs: `rm made`"),
    where2("RENAME", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_hostfs: `mv f moved`"),
    where("CHMOD", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac",
      "native_hostfs: `chmod 000 f; cat f`, `chmod 644 f; cat f`, `chmod 000 sub; ls sub` — asked " +
      "through what the mode *does* rather than through `ls -l`, whose owner and date no two runs " +
      "share. Driven as scripts, so GNU and both hosts answer the same question. This is the " +
      "comparison the `SET_EXECUTABLE` entry below says a script case could not reach, and the reason " +
      "it could not was that there was no `chmod` applet; `issues/system/0296c` added the capability " +
      "and the applet together, so the shell route reaches this one from the start"),
    where("SET_EXECUTABLE", "packages/platform/test/wac/setexecutable_test.wac",
      "setexecutable: a program built for both hosts, run on a file whose starting mode the test pins " +
      "with `chmod`, and the resulting mode compared — `setexecutable_test.wac`. **This entry was a " +
      "gap and the gap was real.** It said the arithmetic is duplicated and only the Deno copy " +
      "exercised, so a wrong mask elsewhere would not be caught; on 2026-08-24 the V8 native host was " +
      "setting `mode | 0o100` and clearing `mode & !0o100` where the other three do " +
      "`mode | ((mode & 0o444) >> 2)` and clear `0o111`. Measured 744 against 755, and 655 against " +
      "644 on the way back. The capability's only other oracle, `checkout_test.wac`, passed " +
      "throughout — git reads any execute bit as 100755 — which is why a sharper check on a " +
      "neighbouring question is not coverage of this one. A shell case could not reach it: " +
      "native_hostfs drives the hosts through scripts and there is no `chmod` applet. " +
      "issues/system/0132"),
    where2("EXEC", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac",
      "native: one module built once and run by both native binaries — `native_test.wac`'s " +
      "`test_running_a_host_program_agrees_between_the_two_native_hosts`, which compares the three " +
      "verdicts `exec_probe.wac` prints (an inherited stream, an added environment, a cleared one) " +
      "and the grandchild's line on the other stream. `exec_test.wac` drives the V8 host on its own, " +
      "with cases run *twice* — once with `--allow-run` where they pass and once without where they " +
      "fail, which is what says the grant gates rather than decorates — and `runtimes_test.wac` runs " +
      "the same probe on the Deno and Node hosts and compares those two with each other, so all four " +
      "implementations are now reached. " +
      "**This was a gap until 2026-08-20 and it cost twice.** The arithmetic is written three times " +
      "(Rust twice, Deno once) and all three had the same deadlock: stdin was written to the end " +
      "before either output pipe was read, so a child that answers while it is being fed — `cat`, " +
      "`grep`, any filter — blocked on its own output past the pipe buffer while the host blocked on " +
      "the write, and two megabytes through `cat` hung every one of them. Then both Rust hosts ran " +
      "the child inside the capability call, so the ticket came back already answered and two `exec` " +
      "calls could not overlap however they were written — three one-second sleeps took 3012ms to " +
      "submit and 1ms to wait, against the Deno host's 8ms and 1001ms. Neither was found by a row " +
      "here; the first came from batching a corpus through `grep`, the second from porting a test " +
      "that ran its cases three at a time. **A third arrived the day the row was closed**: the Node " +
      "host raised EPIPE as an unhandled event when `/bin/echo` exited without reading its stdin, " +
      "which killed the host — nothing had run `echo` through it before. issues/system/0165, 0173, " +
      "0182, 0198, 0211"),
    where("OPEN_FILE", "packages/platform/test/wac/stdhandles_test.wac",
      "stdhandles: `closeout_probe.wac` writes, appends to and reads back one file through `Files.openWrite`, " +
      "`openAppend` and `openRead`, run on the V8 binary and built for Deno and Node, each compared with " +
      "the same expected lines; and native_hostfs's `cat < f` and `echo new > made`, which since design/system/0013 " +
      "step 6 are this request and no longer a redirect of the program's own streams. Not yet built for the " +
      "wasmtime host by stdhandles"),
    sameAs("CLOSE_FILE", "OPEN_FILE", "as OPEN_FILE: every stream the probe opens it closes, and reads back after"),
    where("CLOSE_OUTPUT", "packages/platform/test/wac/stdhandles_test.wac",
      "stdhandles: the same probe writes `a`, closes its output twice, and writes again — `a` alone on " +
      "its output and `refused` on its diagnostic output on each of the three hosts. It does not show " +
      "the reader seeing the end *before* the program exits, which an `exec` cannot tell apart"),
    where("READ_CHUNK", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac",
      "native_hostfs: `cat sub/deep > out` streams, and 300 KB through stdin — past one chunk, which " +
      "is the only case that makes `readChunk` loop"),

    // ── The system a session is ──
    where("SPAWN_SELF", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac",
      "native_hostfs: `kill %1` ends a background job on both; native_shell runs a sealed session " +
      "whose every command is a spawned child, so all three of a child's handles are exercised rather " +
      "than only its output"),
    where("EXIT_CODE", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "native_hostfs: the status of every AGREED script"),
    where2("CLOSE_SOCKET", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/node_net_test.wac", "native_hostfs: `kill %1` is delivered by closing the child"),
    where("CLOSE_SEND", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_examples_test.wac",
      "native_examples: `halfclose` runs both ends in one program and prints two lines — the " +
      "server reaching `End`, and the client still reading afterwards. One line alone is passed " +
      "by a wrong host in each direction: a no-op fails the first, a full close the second"),
    where("CLOSE_FEED", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_shell_test.wac",
      "native_shell: pipelines in the corpus slice — and native_examples' `feed`, which is the only " +
      "place the *distinction* from `closeSocket` is asked on both hosts: the child has to stay alive " +
      "to say what it heard, which a stop would prevent"),
    where("CWD", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "native_hostfs: every script `cd`s first, by design"),
    where2("WRITE_STDOUT", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_shell_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_shell: 25 corpus scripts, byte for byte"),
    where2("WRITE_STDERR", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_hostfs: stderr compared on every AGREED script"),
    where2("ARG_COUNT", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_shell_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_shell: `sealedsh -c` passes its script as an argument"),
    where2("ARG", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_shell_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac", "native_shell: same"),
    where2("READ_STDIN", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_hostfs_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac",
      "native_hostfs: ten scripts with real piped input — this gap is what found the bug that every " +
      "filter under a spawning shell read nothing on the native host"),

    // ── Named gaps. Each says what makes it hard, because that is the next person's starting point ──
    where("NOW_MILLIS", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_examples_test.wac",
      "native_examples: `clocks` asks the *shape* rather than the value, which is the only thing two " +
      "clocks can agree on — this one dates, so the claim is that it is past a moment already gone. " +
      "The gap here used to say a comparison was impossible by construction; what was missing was a " +
      "program that compared the promises instead of the readings"),
    where2("MONOTONIC_NANOS", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_examples_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac",
      "native_examples: `clocks` reads it twice and requires the second not to be earlier — equal is " +
      "fine, a coarse clock read inside one microsecond says so — and separately requires it not to " +
      "be a plausible epoch stamp, which is what a host answering the wall clock in disguise would " +
      "produce while passing everything else"),
    where("SLEEP_MILLIS", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_examples_test.wac",
      "native_examples: `clocks` brackets a 50ms sleep between two monotonic reads and asks two " +
      "things — it lasted at least as long as asked, and the two hosts said the same. **No upper bound**: a " +
      "sleep that overran is a loaded machine, not a broken host, and bounding it would measure the " +
      "machine — `hostfs.wac` makes the same argument at greater length"),
    where("RANDOM_BYTES", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_examples_test.wac",
      "native_examples: `entropy` walks the documented range — 1 byte, the 65,536-byte Web Crypto " +
      "cap, one byte past it, and the megabyte ceiling — and asks of each that the length came back " +
      "and that a large block is not one repeated byte, which is what an unfilled chunk looks like. " +
      "The bytes are random by definition and are not compared. This entry used to rest on `head -c " +
      "16 /dev/urandom | wc -c` in native_shell, which still runs and still says a sealed session " +
      "gets a CSPRNG **without a grant** — but sixteen bytes is on the near side of every boundary, " +
      "and 0122 was two hosts throwing above 64 KiB inside a range all three of them bounds-checked " +
      "as legal"),
    where2("ENV", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_shell_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/runtimes_test.wac",
      "native_shell: the seal, on both hosts. `sealing.test.ts` had asked it on one, which is half a " +
      "claim about a design whose whole point is that the same system appears on two — and the two " +
      "runtimes reach the environment by completely different routes. Both binaries are built " +
      "**with** the env grant and a session still sees none of it, with a canary that the grant is " +
      "live on each: an unsealed shell prints the machine's `$HOME` on both, or the seal below would " +
      "be passing against a runtime that never reads the environment for anybody"),
    where("CONNECT", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_examples_test.wac",
      "native_examples: `writeread` listens, accepts and connects to itself on loopback, so one " +
      "program exercises all three on both hosts and the transcripts are compared. The port differs " +
      "by construction and is masked; it is allocated per run by `daemon.wac`'s `Held` rather than " +
      "fixed, because these run in the parallel lane. Before this the network was exercised end to " +
      "end by `arrival_users` over ssh — but only with the native host as the *server*, so nothing " +
      "compared the three calls themselves"),
    sameAs2("LISTEN", "CONNECT", "packages/platform/test/wac/node_net_test.wac", "as CONNECT — the same program, the same run"),
    where2("BIND_DATAGRAM", "packages/platform/test/wac/datagram_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/echod_test.wac",
      "datagram: one program binds two sockets on loopback, sends between them and reads what " +
      "arrives, so one run exercises all three calls — `datagram_test.wac`, the shape `writeread` " +
      "uses for connect/listen/accept. Built for the Deno host and the V8 native one and compared, " +
      "with the **wasmtime** host asked for as well because that is the one `issues/lang/0109` " +
      "reported. Port 0 and read the port back, since these run in the parallel lane. " +
      "**This entry was a gap for a reason that had stopped being true.** It said the capability " +
      "itself was missing, because three more fields on `Cli` would put every native program past " +
      "sixteen funcrefs per signature. 0109 closed on 2026-08-13 — not by raising the sixteen, which " +
      "is still sixteen, but by registering `Pending<T>`'s `settled` and `drop` once instead of once " +
      "per instantiation, since neither depends on `T`. `Cli` has carried the three fields since and " +
      "`packages/webrtc` and `packages/quic` use them; all four hosts printed the same transcript the " +
      "first time they were asked. What was left was nobody having written the comparison"),
    sameAs("RECEIVE_FROM", "BIND_DATAGRAM", "as BIND_DATAGRAM — the same program, the same run"),
    sameAs("SEND_TO", "BIND_DATAGRAM", "as BIND_DATAGRAM"),
    sameAs2("ACCEPT", "CONNECT", "packages/platform/test/wac/node_net_test.wac", "as CONNECT"),
    where("RECV", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_shell_test.wac",
      "native_shell: every filesystem operation of every spawned stage. `sealedsh` spawns its stages " +
      "and serves their filesystem over a handle (wac-mono 0116), so a corpus script that reads a " +
      "file is `send` and `recv` in both directions on both hosts — which is why this stopped being a " +
      "gap without a test being written for it"),
    where2("SEND", "packages/platform/test/wac/native_shell_test.wac", "packages/platform/test/wac/node_net_test.wac",
      "native_shell: as RECV, and the same traffic — plus native_examples' `feed`, which asks the " +
      "part a pipeline never reaches: what the **bool** says. `send` answers `Pending<bool>` and three " +
      "JavaScript hosts were not answering it at all (`kid.in.push(...)` with the result discarded), " +
      "so a send after `closeFeed` reported success and dropped the bytes — 0121, and 0120 in the " +
      "same line. The example also asks the other direction, where the native host was the one out of " +
      "step: its queues had no cap, so a child writing nine megabytes into a parent that would not " +
      "read never waited, while every JavaScript host held it at eight"),
    gap("SPAWN",
      "**not implemented in the native runtime**, which is the answer rather than a missing test. " +
      "What `spawn` takes is a program's *source*, and on the JavaScript hosts that means a worker " +
      "bundle — an artifact no wasmtime host could run whatever it did with it. `native/src/main.rs` " +
      "answers -1 with a reason (not -2, which would mean this world cannot spawn at all and would " +
      "make the shell give up on `spawnSelf` too, which works). So there is nothing here for two " +
      "hosts to agree *about*, and what is compared instead is everything around it: native_hostfs " +
      "runs a path that is not a program on both and pins each runtime's sentence, and the three " +
      "cases the *filesystem* settles — missing, a directory, a path in a pipeline — are compared " +
      "against GNU on both hosts, where all of them used to be `command not found` and 127"),
    // ── Withdrawing a request ──
    where("DROP", "packages/platform/test/wac/droppedchild_test.wac",
      "droppedchild: a spawn nobody waited for, dropped — the child stops on both native hosts, and with " +
      "`detached` it runs on, which is the arm that says the stop was the drop's. lostbytes_test drops a connect " +
      "and a read of the program's own input the same way. This is the rule settled before step 6: an answer " +
      "nobody has read is still the host's (design/system/0013, `issues/system/0370a`)"),

    // ── Modules ──
    where("LOAD", "packages/platform/test/wac/load_test.wac",
      "load: `loadee.wac` loaded and each of its shapes called, directly on the binary running the file and " +
      "built as `loadprobe.wac` for Deno and Node, every host held to one expected output"),
    sameAs("CALL", "LOAD", "as LOAD: what is compared is what each call answered"),
    sameAs("UNLOAD", "LOAD", "as LOAD: the probe unloads what it loaded"),
    gap("VALIDATE",
      "nothing compares it. Its one caller is `wac` itself, which asks the engine about a module it just built; " +
      "the native hosts answer from two different engines, so the case worth having is a module one of them " +
      "refuses and the other accepts")
  ];
}

Cover? entryFor(Cover[] all, string op) {
  for (i32 i = 0; i < all.len(); i++) { if (all[i].op == op) { return all[i]; } }
  return null;
}

export string test_the_readmes_request_table_lists_every_request_there_is(Sys sys) {
  Files fs = sys.files();
  // The table in `packages/platform/README.md` is the first thing a person reads to find out what a
  // host must answer, and it is the kind of list that goes quietly short: three capabilities had been
  // added to `platform.wac` without it, each by a different piece of work, none of which was looking at
  // a README.
  //
  // `tools/wac/docsignatures_test.wac` checks that every name a README *quotes* exists. This is the other
  // direction, which nothing had: that every request that exists is quoted. A reader cannot tell a
  // request that is missing from the table from one that does not exist.
  T t = T.create();
  string src = sourceOf(fs, "packages/platform/README.md");
  i32 from = src.indexOf("| op | request |");
  t.isTrue(from >= 0, "the request table is gone from the README");
  if (from < 0) { return t.report(); }
  i32 span = src.slice(from, src.len()).indexOf("\n\n");
  string table = src.slice(from, span < 0 ? src.len() : from + span);
  string[] all = requests(fs);
  string missing = "";
  for (i32 i = 0; i < all.len(); i++) {
    if (!contains(table, "`\{all[i]}`")) { missing = missing + (missing == "" ? "" : " ") + all[i]; }
  }
  t.eqStr(missing, "", "numbered in std and absent from the README's table");
  return t.report();
}

export string test_the_bridge_numbers_every_request_as_std_does(Sys sys) {
  Files fs = sys.files();
  // **Two tables of one numbering.** std's `OP_*` is what a program sends; `host/ops.ts` is what the JavaScript
  // bridge carries between a worker and its host. A request numbered differently on the two sides arrives as a
  // different request, and every call goes somewhere plausible.
  T t = T.create();
  string[] std = requestNumbers(fs);
  string[] bridge = bridgeNumbers(fs);
  t.isTrue(std.len() >= 40, "only \{std.len()} requests found in std — has the extraction broken?");
  t.isTrue(bridge.len() >= 40, "only \{bridge.len()} opcodes found in ops.ts — has the extraction broken?");
  string differ = "";
  for (i32 i = 0; i < std.len(); i++) {
    // `DROP` is std's alone: the bridge withdraws a request by cancelling its slot, not by sending one.
    if (std[i] == "DROP=0") { continue; }
    if (!has(bridge, std[i])) { differ = differ + (differ == "" ? "" : " ") + std[i]; }
  }
  t.eqStr(differ, "", "requests ops.ts does not number as std does");
  return t.report();
}

export string test_every_request_std_numbers_the_host_with_no_js_answers(Sys sys) {
  Files fs = sys.files();
  // design/0001's aim is that the same programs run on a bootable kernel-and-wasmtime stack *and*
  // without it. That makes "is anything in the JavaScript hosts load-bearing that the wasm side cannot
  // supply?" a question with a definite answer, and this is it: nothing.
  //
  // The known exception is behavioural rather than structural and is covered elsewhere: `spawn` of a
  // *foreign* program answers with a reason on the native host (see `SPAWN` above).
  T t = T.create();
  string[] served = nativeRequests(fs);
  string[] all = requests(fs);
  // The counts first: an extraction that quietly matched nothing would make the loop below vacuous,
  // which is how this test would come to certify a surface it never read.
  t.isTrue(served.len() >= 30, "only \{served.len()} native requests found — has the extraction broken?");
  t.isTrue(all.len() >= 40, "only \{all.len()} requests found in std — has the extraction broken?");
  string missing = "";
  for (i32 i = 0; i < all.len(); i++) {
    if (!has(served, all[i])) { missing = missing + (missing == "" ? "" : " ") + all[i]; }
  }
  t.eqStr(missing, "",
          "the native runtime has no arm for these, so a program that asks gets no answer it can read");
  return t.report();
}

/** The requests both hosts have to answer — those the native host has an arm for. */
string[] sharedOpcodes(string[] ops, string[] caps) {
  string[] out = string[].filled(ops.len(), "");
  i32 n = 0;
  for (i32 i = 0; i < ops.len(); i++) {
    if (has(caps, ops[i])) { out[n] = ops[i]; n = n + 1; }
  }
  string[] trimmed = string[].filled(n, "");
  for (i32 i = 0; i < n; i++) { trimmed[i] = out[i]; }
  return trimmed;
}

export string test_every_opcode_on_the_two_host_surface_is_accounted_for(Sys sys) {
  Files fs = sys.files();
  T t = T.create();
  string[] ops = requests(fs);
  string[] caps = nativeRequests(fs);
  t.isTrue(ops.len() >= 40, "only \{ops.len()} requests found — has the extraction broken?");

  string[] shared = sharedOpcodes(ops, caps);
  string[] jsOnly = string[].filled(ops.len() - shared.len(), "");
  i32 p = 0;
  for (i32 i = 0; i < ops.len(); i++) {
    if (!has(caps, ops[i])) { jsOnly[p] = ops[i]; p = p + 1; }
  }

  // The derivation itself, asserted: a mapping that silently stopped matching would report the whole
  // surface as JavaScript-only and this file would have nothing to say while looking complete.
  t.isTrue(shared.len() >= 35,
           "only \{shared.len()} shared opcodes — has the mapping broken?");
  // **Empty, written down.** Every opcode the JavaScript hosts answer has a native arm: the twelve
  // that did not were std's `Page`, deleted in design/system/0011 step 7. A capability the native
  // host *dropped* would arrive here, so this stays a written expectation rather than a derived one.
  t.eqStr(joined(sorted(jsOnly), " "), "",
          "an opcode with no native arm — a capability the native host dropped, or one only JavaScript has");

  Cover[] all = coverage();
  string unlisted = "";
  for (i32 i = 0; i < shared.len(); i++) {
    if (entryFor(all, shared[i]) is null) {
      unlisted = unlisted + (unlisted == "" ? "" : ", ") + shared[i];
    }
  }
  t.eqStr(unlisted, "",
          "opcodes with no entry in the ledger. A new capability needs a comparison on both hosts, " +
          "or an entry saying what makes one hard — quietly uncovered is the one option this file " +
          "exists to stop");

  string listedButGone = "";
  for (i32 i = 0; i < all.len(); i++) {
    if (!has(shared, all[i].op)) {
      listedButGone = listedButGone + (listedButGone == "" ? "" : ", ") + all[i].op;
    }
  }
  t.eqStr(listedButGone, "", "the ledger names opcodes that are not on the shared surface");
  return t.report();
}

export string test_the_ledger_says_how_much_of_the_surface_is_actually_compared(Sys sys) {
  Output err = sys.stderr();
  Output out = sys.stdout();
  Env env = sys.env();
  Files fs = sys.files();
  Procs procs = sys.procs();
  T t = T.create();
  string[] shared = sharedOpcodes(requests(fs), nativeRequests(fs));
  Cover[] all = coverage();
  i32 covered = 0;
  i32 withNode = 0;
  i32 withoutV8 = 0;
  for (i32 i = 0; i < shared.len(); i++) {
    Cover? c = entryFor(all, shared[i]);
    if (!(c is not null) || !isCompared(c!)) { continue; }
    covered = covered + 1;
    string hosts = hostsOf(err, env, fs, procs, all, c!);
    if (contains(hosts, "node")) { withNode = withNode + 1; }
    if (!contains(hosts, "v8")) { withoutV8 = withoutV8 + 1; }
  }

  // **Printed, not merely asserted.** A number nobody sees is a number nobody acts on, and the point
  // of the ledger is that the gap is visible from a normal run rather than by reading this file.
  //
  // **Three numbers, because one was read as answering a question it does not.** `N of 44 have a
  // comparison` was true and was taken for *the hosts agree*; there are four hosts and no entry
  // compares all of them. The two below are derived from what the tests spawn, so they cannot drift
  // from the tests the way a written-down host list would — `issues/system/0268a`.
  out.line("    conformance: \{covered} of \{shared.len()}" +
           " opcodes have a comparison; \{shared.len() - covered} are named gaps");
  out.line("                 \{withNode} include the Node host; \{withoutV8}" +
           " do not include the V8 binary");

  // A floor rather than a target: this is allowed to go up without editing the test, and a change
  // that takes it *down* — a comparison deleted, an opcode added with a gap entry and forgotten — has
  // to be deliberate.
  t.isTrue(covered >= 24,
           "only \{covered} opcodes are compared across hosts; this was 19 when the " +
           "ledger was written");
  return t.report();
}

/**
 * Every entry that claims a comparison names something that says which hosts it drives.
 *
 * **This is what makes `hostsOf` an instrument rather than a guess.** It reads call-shaped patterns
 * out of a test file, and a derive like that under-reports the moment a file reaches a host by a
 * spelling the pattern list does not have — silently, and in the direction that looks like less
 * coverage rather than a broken tool. Mine did exactly that on its first draft:
 * `native_shell_test.wac` came back Deno-only because it reaches the binary through
 * `nativeBinaryPath` and the list had only `wacBinary` and `binaryPath`.
 *
 * So an entry deriving **no host at all** is a failure here rather than a quiet zero. Same shape as
 * `tools/wac/links_test.wac`'s *"has the scan stopped matching?"* floor, and for the same reason: a
 * scan that finds nothing looks identical to a tree with nothing in it.
 *
 * A `sameAs` is checked too, which nothing did when it was the prose `"as CONNECT"`: the opcode it
 * borrows has to exist and has to be a `where`, so a rename cannot leave four rows pointing at
 * nothing while still counting as covered.
 */
export string test_every_entry_names_a_file_that_says_which_hosts_it_drives(Sys sys) {
  Output err = sys.stderr();
  Env env = sys.env();
  Files fs = sys.files();
  Procs procs = sys.procs();
  T t = T.create();
  Cover[] all = coverage();
  string[] silent = string[].filled(all.len(), "");
  i32 n = 0;
  i32 distinct = 0;
  string everySeen = "";
  for (i32 i = 0; i < all.len(); i++) {
    if (!isCompared(all[i])) { continue; }
    if (all[i].kind == "sameAs") {
      bool found = false;
      for (i32 j = 0; j < all.len(); j++) {
        if (all[j].op == all[i].ref && all[j].kind == "where") { found = true; }
      }
      t.isTrue(found, "\{all[i].op} borrows \{all[i].ref}, which is not a `where` entry");
    }
    string hosts = hostsOf(err, env, fs, procs, all, all[i]);
    if (hosts == "") { silent[n] = all[i].op; n = n + 1; continue; }
    everySeen = everySeen + "+\{hosts}";
  }
  t.eqI32(n, 0, "these entries name a test that drives no host this can recognise — a spelling the "
                + "pattern list is missing, not an absence of coverage: " + joined(silent, ", "));

  // **A partial miss, which the per-entry floor cannot see** — and this is not hypothetical, it
  // happened three times while the derive was being written. An entry deriving `deno` when the file
  // drives Deno *and* Node passes a check that only refuses deriving *nothing*. So the derive is
  // cross-checked against an independent signal: any backing test whose source spawns a host must be
  // credited with it. Different question, different scan, and the two have to agree.
  // **Both files, and the first version of this checked only the first.** The Node coverage lives in
  // the `also` test for every entry that has any, so scanning `ref` alone found nothing to complain
  // about and the check passed while deriving zero Node — proved by removing the `cli.exec("node"`
  // pattern and watching this stay green. A cross-check that cannot see where the answer comes from
  // is not a cross-check.
  for (i32 i = 0; i < all.len(); i++) {
    if (all[i].kind == "gap") { continue; }
    string hosts = hostsOf(err, env, fs, procs, all, all[i]);
    string[] files = [all[i].kind == "where" ? all[i].ref : "", all[i].also];
    for (i32 f = 0; f < files.len(); f++) {
      if (files[f] == "") { continue; }
      string src = sourceOf(fs, files[f]);
      if (contains(src, "cli.exec(\"node\"") && !contains(hosts, "node")) {
        t.fail("\{all[i].op}: \{files[f]} spawns node and the derive did not say so");
      }
      if (contains(src, "cli.exec(\"deno\"") && !contains(hosts, "deno")) {
        t.fail("\{all[i].op}: \{files[f]} spawns deno and the derive did not say so");
      }
    }
  }

  // **And the whole list going stale at once**, which the per-entry floor cannot see: if every
  // pattern stopped matching, every entry would fail above — but if the *tests* were rewritten onto
  // one host, each would still derive one and the ledger would quietly become single-host.
  string[] names = ["wasmtime", "v8", "deno", "node"];
  for (i32 i = 0; i < names.len(); i++) {
    if (contains(everySeen, names[i])) { distinct = distinct + 1; }
  }
  t.isTrue(distinct >= 3,
           "the whole ledger derives only \{distinct} distinct host(s) — it compared "
           + "wasmtime, v8 and deno when this was written");
  return t.report();
}
